{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% macro shot(n, alt, eager=false, cls="") -%}
{%- set d = shots[n] -%}
<img class="th-light {{ cls }}" src="{{ root }}static/shots/{{ n }}-light.webp" width="{{ d.w }}" height="{{ d.h }}" alt="{{ alt }}" loading="{{ 'eager' if eager else 'lazy' }}"{% if eager %} fetchpriority="high"{% endif %}>
<img class="th-dark {{ cls }}" src="{{ root }}static/shots/{{ n }}-dark.webp" width="{{ d.w }}" height="{{ d.h }}" alt="{{ alt }}" loading="lazy">
{%- endmacro %}
{% block main %}
<section class="hero hero-xl">
  <a class="pill" href="#new"><b>New</b> v{{ version }} · always-fresh reports, cost drill-down, self-explaining charts ›</a>
  <p class="eyebrow">FinOps for AWS, from your terminal</p>
  <h1>See every dollar.<br><span class="grad">Act on every insight.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">spendlens finds untagged resources, oversized instances, idle waste and unexpected cost spikes, puts a
    price on each one, and answers your questions in plain English. Read-only, by design.</p>
  <div class="ctas">
    <a class="btn primary" href="#install">Install</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="#screenshots">Take the tour ›</a>
  </div>
  <figure class="product">
    <div class="window">
      <div class="window-bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i><span>report.html · spendlens dashboard</span></div>
      {{ shot("hero", "The spendlens dashboard: spend this month, flagged cost changes, projected month end, potential saving, tag compliance and anomalies", eager=true) }}
    </div>
    <figcaption>One self-contained HTML report. Works offline, in CI artifacts, and in print. Shown with demo data.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="section new-in" id="new">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">New in v{{ version }}</p>
    <h2 class="h2">Always complete.<br><span class="grad">Always explained.</span></h2>
    <p class="sub">Reports that are never half-empty, cost changes you can trace to the resource, and every chart
      telling you how to read it.</p>
    <div class="bento new-bento">
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["clock"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Always fresh</p>
        <h3>Never half-empty.</h3>
        <p>The dashboard, Slack and Teams summaries, and the savings total reuse every check already run today and
          run the rest first. Every report covers everything, and is at most a day old.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens dashboard -o report.html
spendlens dashboard --refresh -o report.html
spendlens dashboard --source last -o report.html</code></pre>
        <p class="t-foot">Same for notify and savings summary · reuse, re-run all, or no AWS calls</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["flow"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Cost drill-down</p>
        <h3>From "EC2 went up" to which app.</h3>
        <p>Break every service's change down by a second level: a tag such as Name or Team, usage type or region.
          Add <code>-f html</code> for a searchable report.</p>
        <pre class="t-code"><code>spendlens cost diff -g service -b tag:Name
spendlens cost diff -g service -b usage-type
spendlens cost diff -g usage-type --top 15</code></pre>
        <p class="t-foot">Works with months, ranges and month to date</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["book"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Self-explaining</p>
        <h3>"How to read this", everywhere.</h3>
        <p>Every dashboard tab and report says what it shows, how it's calculated with your thresholds, and what to
          do next. "Flagged" always states its rule.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["chart"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Charted reports</p>
        <h3>Charts for every cost command.</h3>
        <p><code>-f html</code> on cost diff, trend, showback, anomalies and forecast gives bar, line and budget
          charts, with the full table.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["list"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Findings at scale</p>
        <h3>Tens of thousands of results.</h3>
        <p>Filter, search and sort every check, with its thresholds, why each resource alarmed, and its current
          value.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile">
        <span class="tile-ico" aria-hidden="true">{{ icons["pulse"]|safe }}</span>
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Live progress</p>
        <h3>No more silent waits.</h3>
        <p>Long runs show the current step and an elapsed timer. eu-west-1 by default, any regions you choose.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="ctas new-ctas">
      <a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}cheatsheet/">Open the cheat sheet</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ repo_url }}/releases/tag/v{{ version }}">Release notes ›</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">The highlights.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Your whole AWS estate, checked against your company's standards and priced, from a single
      <code>finops.yaml</code>.</p>
    <div class="bento">
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Overview</p>
        <h3>Everything that matters, on one page.</h3>
        <p>Spend and its change, flagged cost changes, the projected month end, potential savings, tag compliance and
          anomalies.</p>
        <div class="t-shot">{{ shot("kpis", "Dashboard headline tiles: spend $55.4K, 2 flagged changes, $59.1K projected month end, $4,613 a month potential saving, 92.2% tag compliance, 3 anomalies") }}</div>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-num">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Controls</p>
        <b class="big grad">{{ stats.controls }}</b>
        <p>built-in checks for tags, rightsizing and waste, across {{ stats.resource_types }} resource types.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-num">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Safety</p>
        <b class="big">0</b>
        <p>writes to your AWS account. Read-only APIs, read-only role, read-only SQL.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-wide">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Forecast</p>
        <h3>Month-end, without surprises.</h3>
        <p>Projected spend against your budget and every AWS Budget, with a clear status for each.</p>
        <div class="t-shot">{{ shot("forecast", "Month-end forecast against budgets: account forecast over budget, platform-monthly at risk, data-monthly on track") }}</div>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-num">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">AI providers</p>
        <b class="big">{{ stats.providers }}</b>
        <p>Amazon Bedrock, Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI, or any OpenAI-compatible model.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="tile t-num">
        <p class="t-eyebrow">Formats</p>
        <b class="big">4</b>
        <p>table, CSV, JSON and HTML from every command, plus Slack and Teams summaries.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
      <p class="t-eyebrow">Cost diff &amp; trend</p>
      <h2>Know what changed.<br>Before finance asks.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
      <p>Compare any two months, quarters or date ranges, by service, account, region or any tag. New and removed
        services are always shown, changes that cross your thresholds are flagged with the rule stated, and
        <code>-b tag:Name</code> breaks each service's change down to the resource or app behind it.</p>
      <ul class="chips"><li>Month over month</li><li>Month to date</li><li>Quarter vs quarter</li><li>By tag</li><li>Drill-down</li><li>CI gate</li></ul>
      <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/cost/">Cost analysis ›</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ shot("cost", "Biggest cost changes by service: Redshift down $3,710, EC2 up $3,698, Bedrock up $1,660") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase alt">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
      <p class="t-eyebrow">Savings</p>
      <h2>One number.<br>Every dollar explained.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
      <p>spendlens's own rightsizing and waste findings, merged with AWS Cost Optimization Hub and Savings Plan
        coverage. Each resource counts once, AWS's figures win where both exist, and every adjustment is listed.
        Every total covers all four sources and is at most a day old.</p>
      <ul class="chips"><li>De-duplicated</li><li>AWS figures preferred</li><li>Always complete</li><li>Review before acting</li></ul>
      <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/savings/">Savings ›</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ shot("savings", "Savings summary: $4,613 a month potential saving, 65% from spendlens findings and 35% from AWS Cost Optimization Hub") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
      <p class="t-eyebrow">Tags &amp; showback</p>
      <h2>Every dollar,<br>owned.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
      <p>Mandatory, untagged, prohibited, tag-limit and allowed-value checks across {{ stats.resource_types }}
        resource types, with the exact tags each resource is missing. Showback by team and environment calls out the
        untagged share, so it gets fixed at the source.</p>
      <ul class="chips"><li>{{ stats.resource_types }} resource types</li><li>5 checks</li><li>Showback by tag</li><li>Budgets per team</li></ul>
      <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/tags/">Tag compliance ›</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ shot("tags", "Tag compliance by check: mandatory 78.4%, untagged 86.5%, expected values 96.8%, limit and prohibited 100%") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase alt">
  <div class="wrap sc stack">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <div class="sc-title">
      <p class="t-eyebrow">Waste &amp; rightsizing</p>
      <h2>Stop paying<br>for nothing.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="sc-copy">
      <p>Unattached volumes, orphaned snapshots, idle load balancers and NAT gateways, gp2 that should be gp3,
        previous-generation instances, Multi-AZ outside prod. {{ stats.controls - 350 }} rightsizing and waste
        controls, each with a monthly dollar estimate.</p>
      <ul class="chips"><li>EC2 · EBS · RDS · ECS</li><li>Lambda · S3 · ELB</li><li>Graviton</li><li>Extended Support</li></ul>
      <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/rightsize-waste/">Rightsizing &amp; waste ›</a>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">{{ shot("waste", "Idle and orphaned resources with an estimated monthly saving for each") }}</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section showcase ai">
  <div class="wrap sc side">
    <div class="sc-text">
      <p class="t-eyebrow">AI assistant</p>
      <h2>Ask anything.<br>Get answers from your data.</h2>
      <p>Claude on Amazon Bedrock with your own AWS role, or Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure OpenAI or any OpenAI-compatible
        model. Answers cite the run they came from. Every query passes three read-only safety layers, so the assistant
        can look things up but never change anything.</p>
      <ul class="chips"><li>Cites its sources</li><li>Read-only SQL</li><li>Offline from history</li><li>--live when needed</li></ul>
      <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/ai-assistant/">AI assistant ›</a>
    </div>
    <div class="sc-media">
      <div class="terminal" role="img" aria-label="Example: spendlens ask explains why the bill went up last month">
        <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> spendlens ask "Why did our bill go up last month?" --show-tools
<span class="dim">› history_overview · 14 saved runs</span>
<span class="dim">› history_query    · cost_diff run #7 · read-only</span>

Spend rose <span class="hi">+$959 (+1.8%)</span> to $55.4K in October.
  · EC2 grew <span class="hi">+$3,698</span> (+17.6%)
  · Amazon Bedrock grew <span class="hi">+$1,660</span> (+692%)
  · Redshift was decommissioned: <span class="ok">−$3,710</span>
Bedrock and Redshift crossed your alert thresholds.
<span class="dim">Source: cost_diff run #7, 2026-11-09</span></pre>
      </div>
      <p class="terminal-note">Illustrative answer, using the figures in the bundled demo data.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">And so much more.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Every feature is a command. Every command speaks table, CSV, JSON and HTML.</p>
    <div class="ctas" style="margin:-28px 0 44px"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}docs/cli/">Browse every command</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}cheatsheet/">Cheat sheet ›</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}docs/ways-to-run/">Ways to run ›</a></div>
    <div class="grid">
      {% for f in features %}
      <article class="card">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ f.icon|safe }}</div>
        <h3>{{ f.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ f.body }}</p>
        <p><code>{{ f.cmd }}</code></p>
        <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/{{ f.doc }}/">Learn more ›</a>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="screenshots">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Take the tour.</h2>
    <p class="sub">A dashboard you can share, and a terminal that answers the question in front of you. Every screenshot
      below comes from the bundled demo data, so you can reproduce it with <code>--source demo</code>.</p>
    <div class="gallery">
      <div class="seg" role="tablist" aria-label="Screenshots">
        {% for s in screens %}<button type="button" role="tab" id="{{ s.id }}-tab" aria-controls="{{ s.id }}"
          aria-selected="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" tabindex="{{ 0 if loop.first else -1 }}">{{ s.tab }}</button>{% endfor %}
      </div>
      {% for s in screens %}
      <figure class="shot" id="{{ s.id }}" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="{{ s.id }}-tab"{% if not loop.first %} data-hidden{% endif %}>
        <a href="{{ root }}{{ s.src }}" class="frame" aria-label="Open full-size screenshot: {{ s.title }}">
          <img src="{{ root }}{{ s.src }}" width="{{ s.w }}" height="{{ s.h }}" alt="{{ s.title }}: {{ s.caption }}"
               loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}"></a>
        <figcaption><b>{{ s.title }}.</b> {{ s.caption }}</figcaption>
      </figure>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="use-cases">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Built for the questions<br>teams actually ask.</h2>
    <p class="sub">From the weekly platform review to the month-end forecast, spendlens fits how engineering and finance
      already work.</p>
    <div class="cases">
      {% for c in cases %}
      <article class="case">
        <div class="who">{{ c.who }}</div>
        <h3>{{ c.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ c.body }}</p>
        <pre><code>{{ c.cmd }}</code></pre>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Up and running in minutes.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Uses whatever AWS role you already have assumed. No agents, no SaaS, no stored keys.</p>
    <div class="steps two" id="install">
      <div class="step wide"><h3>Install</h3>
        <div class="install-grid">
          <div><h4>macOS / Linux</h4><p>One command installs spendlens, Steampipe, Powerpipe and the AWS plugin. No sudo.</p>
            <pre><code>curl -fsSL {{ site_url }}/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
            <p class="small"><a href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Installer options and manual install ›</a></p></div>
          <div><h4>Docker</h4><p>A ready-made image with everything included, for amd64 and arm64.</p>
            <pre><code>docker pull abhaypratap/spendlens
docker run --rm abhaypratap/spendlens tags --source demo</code></pre>
            <p class="small"><a href="{{ root }}docs/docker/">Running against your account ›</a> · <a href="https://hub.docker.com/r/abhaypratap/spendlens">Docker Hub ›</a></p></div>
        </div></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Describe your standards</h3><p>Mandatory tags, allowed instance types, budgets.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens init
spendlens doctor</code></pre></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Run, review, schedule</h3><p>Try it on sample data first, then point it at your account.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens report --source demo
spendlens init --ci gitlab</code></pre></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="h2">Read-only. By design.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin-bottom:28px">spendlens never creates, changes, tags or deletes anything. Savings are
      proposals for a human to review. In CI it assumes a read-only role through OIDC, so no AWS keys are stored. Its AI
      assistant can only run SQL that passes a parser and then executes on connections that cannot write.</p>
    <div class="ctas"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}architecture/">See the architecture</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM permissions ›</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <h2 class="h2">Questions, answered.</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Can spendlens change anything in my AWS account?</summary>
        <p>No. It only makes read API calls, through Steampipe. Findings and savings are recommendations for a person to
          review, and the <a href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM policy</a> we document grants read-only access.
          The AI assistant's SQL has to pass a parser and then runs on connections that cannot write.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What does it cost to run?</summary>
        <p>spendlens is free and open source under the Apache-2.0 licence, with no subscription and nothing to host. On the AWS side, Cost Explorer API requests cost $0.01
          each. spendlens bounds every query to the periods it needs (typically one request per period), so a full
          <code>spendlens report</code> costs cents. CloudWatch metric reads for rightsizing fall under normal CloudWatch API pricing, which includes a free
          tier. If you use the AI assistant, your LLM provider bills you as usual.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does my data leave my machine?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>spendlens itself sends no telemetry. Data goes only where you send it:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>AWS, for the read calls;</li>
            <li>the LLM provider you configure, and only when you run <code>ask</code>;</li>
            <li>Slack or Teams, and only when you run <code>notify</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>With Amazon Bedrock, the assistant's requests go to Bedrock under your own AWS account.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>Which permissions does it need?</summary>
        <p>A read-only role. The <a href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM permissions</a> page lists every action
          by feature, so you can grant only what you use. In CI it assumes that role through OIDC, so no access keys are
          stored.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it work across multiple AWS accounts?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Point Steampipe at an aggregator connection that spans your accounts and set
          <code>steampipe.connection</code> in <code>finops.yaml</code>. Findings carry their account ID, and
          <code>cost diff -g account</code> breaks spend down by account.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How accurate are the savings estimates?</summary>
        <p>spendlens's own estimates come from unit prices in <code>finops.yaml</code>. The defaults are us-east-1
          list prices; override them for your region or discounts. Use the estimates to rank work, then review. AWS
          Cost Optimization Hub's usage-based figures take priority wherever both exist, and
          <code>savings summary</code> counts each resource once.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I run it on Windows?</summary>
        <p>Yes, with the <a href="{{ root }}docs/docker/">Docker image</a>
          (<code>docker pull abhaypratap/spendlens</code>) or inside WSL. The one-line installer supports macOS and
          Linux.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I add my own checks?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Rules are SQL controls in a Powerpipe mod, and thresholds are parameters. Plugins can add controls,
          commands, savings estimators, AI tools and dashboard tabs without forking. See
          <a href="{{ root }}docs/extending/">Extending</a>.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="h2">Find your first saving<br>in five minutes.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin-bottom:24px">Try it on sample data first; nothing to connect.</p>
    <pre class="cta-code"><code>curl -fsSL {{ site_url }}/install.sh | sh
spendlens report --source demo --out demo-report</code></pre>
    <div class="ctas"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Read the guide</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ repo_url }}">View on GitHub ›</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
